Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.960
And liftoff. The final liftoff of Atlantis. When&nbsp;
NASA retired its Space Shuttle Program in 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:12.160
the U.S. didn't have a way of getting to space&nbsp;
itself. Since the government agency no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.960
developed its own spacecraft, it turned to private&nbsp;
companies for a ride to the International Space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:23.120
Station by creating the Commercial Crew Program.&nbsp;
Think of NASA as a customer looking for an Uber&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:28.800
ride and its Ubers of choice were Boeing and&nbsp;
SpaceX. They've been developing and perfecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:39.440
their infrastructure for a decade before this&nbsp;
moment. Five, four, three two, one zero, liftoff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:46.400
Go NASA, go SpaceX, godspeed Bob and Doug. No&nbsp;
one thought SpaceX would be first to send NASA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:52.160
astronauts into orbit. This is the story of why&nbsp;
Elon Musk's company won the race against Boeing.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.840
The classic tale of David and Goliath is&nbsp;
about a young shepherd who defeats a giant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:05.600
Although many believe the moral of&nbsp;
the story is that the underdog won,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.400
Malcolm Gladwell makes the case in his book that&nbsp;
we're misunderstanding who had the upper hand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:15.440
The giant was the weak one. He was slow&nbsp;
and half-blind. David was powerful because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:21.360
he was quick and agile. SpaceX was the new&nbsp;
kid on the block, the David in the story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:26.880
Boeing was the giant of the aerospace industry&nbsp;
with a legacy stretching back more than 100 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.280
It seems NASA was banking on Boeing when deciding&nbsp;
the amount to give the companies to develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:38.080
spacecraft that would fly astronauts to the&nbsp;
ISS. NASA handed Boeing a $4.2 billion contract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:43.680
compared to $2.6 billion for SpaceX. Its Office&nbsp;
of Inspector General, the oversight division for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:49.200
the agency, put it this way: NASA was willing&nbsp;
to pay $90 million a seat to ride with Boeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:55.440
but only $55 million for a seat with SpaceX,&nbsp;
assuming four astronauts for six missions. Musk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:00.960
pointed out it's not fair that Boeing gets so much&nbsp;
more for the same thing while Boeing disagreed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.600
with the OIG's report, explaining it will fly&nbsp;
the equivalent of a fifth passenger in cargo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:11.360
so the per seat calculation would be less. With&nbsp;
the funding, Boeing manufactured the Starliner.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.440
Please enjoy the tour. This is&nbsp;
its pressure vessel which is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.840
underlying structure of the spacecraft's&nbsp;
crew module that houses the astronauts.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.560
And SpaceX developed the Dragon 2,&nbsp;
an upgrade from its previous version.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:34.480
Hello, everyone. Welcome aboard Dragon. When&nbsp;
astronauts boarded Crew Dragon in May 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:39.760
it meant the space agency no longer had to rely&nbsp;
on the Russians to send Americans into orbit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:45.120
Bob Behnken and Doug Hurley spent about two months&nbsp;
at the ISS conducting science experiments before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.720
returning to Earth. Splashdown. Thanks for flying&nbsp;
SpaceX. Boeing actually had the chance to win the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:58.160
race to space if it were not for an incident in&nbsp;
December 2019. And liftoff. The rise of Starliner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:03.760
and a new era in human spaceflight. But it didn't&nbsp;
quite usher in a new era of human spaceflight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:08.640
The Starliner had a test flight without crew&nbsp;
to see if it was ready to fly astronauts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:13.600
It was supposed to go to the International Space&nbsp;
Station, deliver cargo, and return. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:17.520
there was a software problem, and that&nbsp;
caused the engines to fire at the wrong time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:23.040
and that put Starliner in the wrong orbit so it&nbsp;
never made it to the ISS but it did make it home.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.560
NASA realized it had put too much trust in Boeing&nbsp;
which had a long history of supporting the space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:34.160
agency's missions. The head of NASA's Commercial&nbsp;
Crew Program admitted: "we were, I would say, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:39.760
little more used to the Boeing process." Musk once&nbsp;
put it in a harsher way, telling the Washington&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:45.600
Post Boeing was arrogant and complacent and&nbsp;
doesn't get out of bed for less than $1 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:51.280
Boeing shot back by saying it helped NASA&nbsp;
build the ISS before SpaceX was even born&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:55.840
and "...will deliver on our commitment to&nbsp;
America's journey to Mars. That's what we get out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:01.360
of bed for." While NASA didn't scrutinize Boeing&nbsp;
enough, it paid a lot of attention to SpaceX's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:06.880
software development and that's where SpaceX&nbsp;
shined. Musk came from a software background.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:13.120
He co-founded PayPal and is the CEO of Tesla which&nbsp;
is developing self-driving software. SpaceX's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:18.640
status as a startup also played to its advantage&nbsp;
which might seem counterintuitive but being new to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:25.120
the rocket business meant it had to keep improving&nbsp;
rapidly to stay alive. SpaceX nearly died in 2008&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:30.320
after three failed rocket launches but persevered&nbsp;
for a fourth attempt which was a success.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:40.000
Because of that launch, NASA awarded SpaceX $1.6&nbsp;
billion to resupply cargo to the ISS. Without that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:45.200
contract, SpaceX would have gone bankrupt.&nbsp;
On the other hand, Boeing brings in a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:51.360
of profit every year as the leading producer of&nbsp;
commercial airlines - making $10 billion in 2018.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:56.800
But recently, the company hasn't been doing so&nbsp;
well. The grounding of the 737 Max planes caused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:02.960
Boeing to report a loss in 2019 for the first&nbsp;
time in over 20 years and the pandemic isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:08.400
making it any better. Although space only makes&nbsp;
up a small portion of its annual revenue, for now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.360
that could change in the future. Starliner will&nbsp;
be launched again, possibly later this year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:18.160
and if that goes well there could be a manned&nbsp;
mission to the ISS next year. That would still put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:24.320
Boeing a year behind SpaceX. Regardless of who is&nbsp;
ahead, they both benefit from contracts with NASA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:29.360
because it helps fund their infrastructure that&nbsp;
they can use elsewhere. The Crew Dragon will bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:36.560
three private tourists to the ISS in 2021. You&nbsp;
can take a ride if you have $55 million to spare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:41.520
And more importantly, NASA's funding has&nbsp;
helped SpaceX develop reusable rockets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:46.480
This is where SpaceX has made a name for itself.&nbsp;
The one that launched the Crew Dragon was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:52.400
Falcon 9 - a partially reusable rocket because its&nbsp;
booster lands back on Earth. But things haven't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:57.920
always gone smoothly. In 2015, the Falcon 9&nbsp;
exploded two minutes after liftoff during a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:02.800
resupply mission to the ISS. The following year,&nbsp;
it blew up before it was supposed to launch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:08.560
Facebook's $200 million satellite into orbit.&nbsp;
And landing hasn't always worked out either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:16.560
But those trials appear to be in the past. SpaceX&nbsp;
has landed the Falcon 9 booster many times in one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:22.720
piece, paving the way for the ultimate goal of&nbsp;
developing a fully reusable rocket and spacecraft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:27.920
called Starship. This is, I think, the most&nbsp;
inspiring thing that I've ever seen. The plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:33.040
is to use Starship to send people to the moon,&nbsp;
build a city on Mars, and for travel from one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:38.400
point to another on Earth. Reusability is&nbsp;
crucial for keeping costs low in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:44.560
make space travel possible for everyone. This is&nbsp;
the biggest difference between SpaceX and Boeing:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:50.160
the rocket that launched Boeing's Starliner into&nbsp;
orbit cannot be reused. The Atlas 5 was developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:56.000
by United Launch Alliance or ULA which is a&nbsp;
joint venture between Boeing and Lockheed Martin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:01.200
ULA has mentioned possibly reusing the booster&nbsp;
engines of another rocket, the Vulcan Centaur,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:05.760
by catching them in the air with parachutes&nbsp;
and a helicopter, although the company hasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:11.680
mentioned when testing will begin. Musk recently&nbsp;
called ULA a complete waste of taxpayer money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:17.520
because their rockets are not reusable. It's&nbsp;
clear that SpaceX has a big lead over Boeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:23.200
to the surprise of many industry veterans. But&nbsp;
examined another way, it's not a surprise at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:28.800
SpaceX, like David, is not the underdog. It&nbsp;
has the advantage because it's quick and agile.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:34.720
Goliath never stood a chance. And as of right now,&nbsp;
neither, it seems, does Boeing. Thanks so much for&nbsp;&nbsp;

